#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065321} :activity/id #uuid "f68a8ca8-1207-5a7d-8384-c71245f6f467" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050234 :topic/id #uuid "38146cba-4e2a-55b0-9106-e7c8e4e7b732" :topic/math-academy-id 345} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065385 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a $10\\,\\textrm{ft}$ ladder that is leaning against the wall. A man is pushing the bottom of the ladder towards the wall at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}.$ How fast is the top of the ladder sliding up the wall when the bottom is at a distance of $8\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the wall?\n\nTo solve this and similar problems, we can follow the four steps below.\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram. \n\n![](images/42/42fce00bf688c28024f568b75e60335e40e81dbc266abf64adde28efe235a9f3.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the ladder and the wall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the ladder.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle.\n\n** Step 2:** Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the missing side.\n\nAt the particular moment in question we have $x = 8\\textrm{ ft}$ and $z=10\\textrm{ ft}.$ So, we can find $y$ at this moment using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {8}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 64+{y}^{2} & =100 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n** Step 3:** Identify the variables that change with time.\n\n- The distance of the ladder from the wall ($x$) changes with time at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s},$ which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}.\n$$\n\nThe rate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ is the velocity of the bottom of the ladder. It is negative because the ladder moves towards the wall (so $x$ becomes smaller with time).\n\n- The length of the ladder ($z$) is fixed and doesn't change with time, so\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 0.\n$$\n\n- The distance of the top of the ladder from the ground ($y$) changes with time, and it's what we are looking for:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\textrm{?}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Take the derivative with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nTo find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t},$ we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t,$ using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}-2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values and solve for the unknown rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(6)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(10)(0)-2(8)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{0+16}{12} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the ladder slides up the wall at a rate of approx $1.33 \\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s}$ when the bottom is at a distance of $8\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the wall." :tutorial/id #uuid "519a6401-a713-563f-bb3e-49181cd7bc50" :tutorial/math-academy-id 492 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cd33c842-935a-5ebb-af04-0637b5fac369" :step/math-academy-id 915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064026 :question/id #uuid "b11d3606-7700-5c0f-89da-26b22a0ffd9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-228"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401935}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "2d5ac3b9-0ce8-515d-a75f-8dfe5e48a2ad" :step/math-academy-id 916 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064383 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064027 :question/id #uuid "0284ca7f-ee39-5b9f-87f7-df109aa2200d" :question/math-academy-id "e-229"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329950}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "d0abf134-e37f-5d32-934e-6bd0afc76e75" :step/math-academy-id 920}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates Using the Pythagorean Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064436}]}
